 2.1 Choose $0<\varepsilon<r_+/2$ and use the extension manifold $\overline{\mathcal M}:=\mathbb R_v\times(r_+-\varepsilon,\infty)\times S^2$ with its product smooth structure. Let $\bar\pi:\overline{\mathcal M}\to S^2$ be the sphere projection. For $y=(v,r,p)$, define the product tangent isomorphism $\bar I_y:T_y\overline{\mathcal M}\to\mathbb R\oplus\mathbb R\oplus T_pS^2$ by $\bar I_y(w):=(dv_y(w),dr_y(w),d\bar\pi_y(w))$. Set $\bar T_y:=\bar I_y^{-1}(1,0,0)$ and $\bar\Phi_y:=\bar I_y^{-1}(0,0,\xi_p)$; these are the product lifts of the stationary and axial fields. Let $\bar\gamma:=\bar\pi^*\gamma_{S^2}$, $\eta:=\bar\pi^*(\gamma_{S^2}(\xi,\cdot))$, and $q:=\bar\gamma(\bar\Phi,\bar\Phi)=|\xi|_{\gamma_{S^2}}^2$. These are global smooth tensors or functions; on any local polar chart of the new sphere factor, $\eta=\sin^2\theta\,d\widetilde\phi$ and $q=\sin^2\theta$. Fix $r_0>r_+$ and define $f(r)=\int_{r_0}^r (s^2+a^2)/\Delta(s)\,ds$ and $h(r)=\int_{r_0}^r a/\Delta(s)\,ds$ on $(r_+,\infty)$. Their integrands are smooth because $\Delta>0$: repeated use of [F7] gives all derivatives of $\Delta^{-1}$, and [F8] handles the polynomial products. [F6] gives $f,h\in C^1$, and successive differentiation of $f'=(r^2+a^2)/\Delta$ and $h'=a/\Delta$ shows that both primitives are smooth. Set $v=t+f(r)$ and, on the sphere factor, replace each point $(x,y,z)$ by $R_{h(r)}(x,y,z):=(x\cos h-y\sin h,x\sin h+y\cos h,z)$. Repeated application of [F10] makes this rotation smoothly dependent on $r$, and its inverse is $R_{-h(r)}$. Since $R_{h(r)}$ is a rotation about the same axis as $\xi$, its differential preserves $\xi$; thus the induced exterior diffeomorphism carries $\Phi$ to $\bar\Phi$. It also carries $T$ to $\bar T$. Hence the exterior map is a smooth diffeomorphism, with coordinate Jacobian determinant $1$ on every local polar chart; there $\widetilde\phi=\phi+h(r)$ and the pullback of the original $\xi^\flat$ is $\eta-aq\Delta^{-1}dr$. Substitution in [F1], using $\Sigma^2+a^2q(\Sigma+2Mr)=(r^2+a^2)^2-a^2\Delta q$, gives the global ingoing expression
 $$g=-\frac{\Delta-a^2q}{\Sigma}\,dv\otimes dv+dv\otimes dr+dr\otimes dv+\Sigma\bar\gamma+\frac{a^2(\Sigma+2Mr)}{\Sigma}\eta\otimes\eta-\frac{2aMr}{\Sigma}(dv\otimes\eta+\eta\otimes dv)-a(dr\otimes\eta+\eta\otimes dr).$$
